dge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "d18aca64-f3ac-5a3a-837c-b8cb8fa44772" :step/math-academy-id 41903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192514 :question/id #uuid "fc8ba431-5a97-5f57-9a83-fdcf4ac98d39" :question/math-academy-id "e-11048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436615}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2a951cc5-70fa-5cd6-a379-4cfe861c71d4" :step/math-academy-id 41983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193890 :tutorial/content "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66f2199e-b3f8-5590-80f3-bbac79027096" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6937 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3b7bd9a-f6d4-521a-ab11-4ff2cecfbe07" :step/math-academy-id 41989 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192518 :question/id #uuid "6f7bdbcf-fb8e-5c70-b368-4e73cc94bdb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436614}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "4bae0aea-9643-5e83-9379-45ac0682c9ca" :step/math-academy-id 42238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193907 :tutorial/content "We can also compute partial derivatives of functions with three or even more variables. \n\nFor example, suppose we have the function $f(x,y,z).$ We find its partial derivatives as follows:\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x},$ we differentiate with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z},$ we differentiate with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ *and* $y$ as constants.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c87e2fef-e77f-5c67-a502-0d900d85ee40" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7692 :tutorial/title "Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "1014e39c-1f7c-5989-8916-134b9e90b165" :step/math-academy-id 44640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193660}}] :activity/title "Computing Partial Derivatives Using the Rules of Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192923}]}
